WEBVTT

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:28.020
<v Ira Glass>OK. First, a warning. I am going to talk about math. Don't worry, it won't be bad. 1644, a monk named Marin Mersenne gets obsessed for a while with prime numbers. You remember prime numbers? They're like the atoms of math, indivisible. They cannot be divided by any other number than themselves. So 3 is a prime number. You can only divide it by 3. Versus 4, which you can divide by 2, and you can get 2. Remember? OK. So Mersenne.
<v Ira Glass>好的。首先，有个警告。我要谈谈数学。别担心，不会很糟糕。1644年，一位名叫马林·梅森的修道士对质数着了迷。你还记得质数吗？它们就像数学的原子，不可分割。除了它们自己，不能被任何其他数字整除。所以3是质数。你只能用3去除它。而4不同，你可以用2去除它，得到2。记得吗？好的。所以，梅森。

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:33.150
<v Paul Hoffman>So he had a formula that he thought could predict prime numbers, OK?
<v Paul Hoffman>他有一个他认为可以预测质数的公式，好吗？

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:43.380
<v Ira Glass>This is Paul Hoffman, who wrote about this in his book The Man Who Loved Only Numbers. He says that mathematicians had been searching for a formula like this to find prime numbers for nearly 2,000 years at that point.
<v Ira Glass>这是保罗·霍夫曼，他在他的书《爱上数字的男人》中写过这件事。他说，当时数学家们已经寻找这样的质数公式近2000年了。

00:00:43.380 --> 00:01:05.750
<v Paul Hoffman>Euclid, way, way back, 2,300 years ago, had proved that there's an infinite number of prime numbers. But he gave no formula for how to find them. I mean, they're easy at small numbers. We can do the math in our head. 7's prime. Nothing divides into it. 11's prime. If I give you a really big number, now you're going to have to start calculating, OK? So this monk came up with a formula.
<v Paul Hoffman>欧几里得，早在2300年前，就已经证明了质数有无数个。但他没有给出如何找到它们的公式。我的意思是，对于小数字来说这很简单。我们可以在脑子里进行计算。7是质数。没有什么能整除它。11是质数。如果我给你一个非常大的数字，现在你就得开始计算了，好吗？所以这位修道士想出了一个公式。

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:31.800
<v Ira Glass>Mersenne creates this formula. And he uses it to spit out prime numbers. And one of the prime numbers that he said that he discovered was-- and this is going to sound a little bit technical-- 2 raised to the 67th power-- that is, 2 times 2 times 2 times 2, 67 times-- minus 1. And if that was confusing, all you need to know is this number of Mersenne's, 2 raised to the 67th minus 1, was famous among mathematicians.
<v Ira Glass>梅森创造了这个公式。他用它来推导质数。他说他发现的其中一个质数是——这听起来会有点专业——2的67次方——也就是说，2乘以2乘以2乘以2，乘67次——减去1。如果这让你感到困惑，你只需要知道梅森的这个数字，2的67次方减1，在数学家中很有名。

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:45.790
<v Paul Hoffman>That's how his paper ended. He said it was a prime number. This is 1644. So 250 years later, we're into the 20th century. I think it's 1903. And you have this mathematician that shows up at a mathematical conference--
<v Paul Hoffman>他的论文就是这样结尾的。他说那是一个质数。这是1644年。250年后，我们进入了20世纪。我想是1903年。有一位数学家出现在一个数学会议上——

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:49.360
<v Ira Glass>And who is this? What is the conference? Where are we?
<v Ira Glass>他是谁？这是什么会议？我们在哪里？

00:01:49.360 --> 00:02:05.190
<v Paul Hoffman>It's here in the United States. His name is Frank Nelson Cole. And he gave his talk a very unassuming title. He titled his talk "On the Factorization of Large Numbers." And he went to a blackboard. And he wrote, 2 to the 67th minus 1--
<v Paul Hoffman>就在美国这里。他的名字叫弗兰克·尼尔森·科尔。他给他的演讲起了一个非常低调的标题。他把演讲命名为“关于大数的因式分解”。他走到黑板前。他写下，2的67次方减1——

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.340
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. Does he say anything?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。他说了什么吗？

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.170
<v Paul Hoffman>He says nothing. He says not a word.
<v Paul Hoffman>他什么也没说。他一个字也没说。

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.990
<v Ira Glass>He just walks over to the blackboard and just, like--
<v Ira Glass>他只是走到黑板前，然后，就像——

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:19.490
<v Paul Hoffman>He writes that. And of course, everybody in the audience knows that that's the famous Mersenne prime. And he writes, equals, and then he writes out a 21-digit number--
<v Paul Hoffman>他写下了那个。当然，台下的每个人都知道那是著名的梅森质数。然后他写下“等于”，接着写出了一个21位的数字——

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:25.790
<v Ira Glass>Oh, in other words, when you take 2 and then multiply it by 2 67 times, and then subtract 1, that is this number, 21 digits long. OK.
<v Ira Glass>哦，换句话说，当你用2乘以2，乘67次，然后减去1，就是这个21位长的数字。好的。

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:40.550
<v Paul Hoffman>Exactly, exactly. Equals this number. Then he moved over to a blank piece of blackboard. And he wrote down two numbers. One is a nine-digit number, times a 12-digit number. He writes those two numbers out.
<v Paul Hoffman>没错，没错。等于这个数字。然后他移到黑板的空白处。他写下了两个数字。一个是九位数，乘以一个十二位数。他把那两个数字写了出来。

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.220
<v Ira Glass>OK, so that's two numbers that were sitting there on the board, multiplication problem, and?
<v Ira Glass>好的，所以黑板上写着两个数字，是个乘法题，然后呢？

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:54.170
<v Paul Hoffman>And then he did the multiplication, just like the way they taught us back in second grade to do it. 7 times 1, he put down the 7. He went through the whole steps--
<v Paul Hoffman>然后他进行了乘法运算，就像我们在小学二年级学的那样。7乘以1，他写下7。他完成了整个过程——

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.440
<v Ira Glass>Just long multiplication. Does he say anything?
<v Ira Glass>只是长乘法。他说话了吗？

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.260
<v Paul Hoffman>Says not a word. Everybody sits there silently.
<v Paul Hoffman>一个字没说。大家静静地坐着。

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:30.460
<v Ira Glass>Now, remember, the whole idea of a prime number is you should not be able to take two numbers, and then multiply them together and get a prime number as a result. It's supposed to be indivisible. If you multiplied two numbers together and you got this 21-digit number as a result, then that 21-digit number is not prime. And if Mersenne thought it was prime-- which he did-- his formula supposedly spits out prime numbers, this one of them, then his formula, 250 years old, is just wrong.
<v Ira Glass>现在记住，质数的全部概念就是你不应该能取两个数字，把它们乘在一起，得到一个质数作为结果。它应该是不可分割的。如果你把两个数字乘在一起得到了这个21位数的数字，那么这个21位数的数字就不是质数。如果梅森认为它是质数——他确实是这样认为的——他的公式理应推导出质数，这是其中之一，那么他250年前的公式就是错的。

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:54.110
<v Ira Glass>So, picture it. There's Frank Nelson Cole at the blackboard, slowly doing long multiplication, these two huge numbers. It takes a while. They're big numbers. It takes minutes, as this room full of mathematicians just watches him, lots of them, I'm sure, scrutinizing him for any math errors. He still has not said a word. And then, he gets to his result.
<v Ira Glass>所以，想象一下。弗兰克·尼尔森·科尔在黑板前，慢慢地做着长乘法，这两个巨大的数字。这需要一点时间。它们是很大的数字。花了几分钟，全屋子的数学家都在看着他，我敢肯定其中许多人在仔细检查他是否有数学错误。他仍然一言未发。然后，他得出了结果。

00:03:54.110 --> 00:04:14.770
<v Paul Hoffman>And indeed, it ends up being that 21-digit number. Now, the whole place erupts into applause. Legend has it, this is the first time at a math conference that people got up and applauded. And he just returns to his seat without a word.
<v Paul Hoffman>而且确实，结果就是那个21位的数字。现在，全场爆发出掌声。传说这是数学会议上第一次人们站起来鼓掌。而他只是默默地回到座位上。

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:27.680
<v Paul Hoffman>And then later, someone asked him, "How long did it actually take you to figure out that Mersenne was wrong, that indeed this number has two factors?" And he said that he spent three years of Sundays working on this.
<v Paul Hoffman>后来，有人问他：“你到底花了多长时间才弄清楚梅森是错的，这个数字确实有两个因子？”他说他花了三个周日的时间研究这个问题。

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:59.280
<v Ira Glass>Three years of Sundays. Paul says these three years of Sundays were probably spent solving the problem by trying every possible solution-- dividing that huge number, 2 to the 67th power minus 1, by one number and then the next number and then the next. Three years of Sundays is 156 Sundays. For 155 of them, Frank Nelson Cole failed. Until finally, on the 156th Sunday, Frank Nelson Cole found a number that would divide it evenly, which, Paul says, is par for the course.
<v Ira Glass>三个周日。保罗说这三个周日大概是他通过尝试每一种可能的解法来解决问题的——用一个数字去除那个巨大的数字，2的67次方减1，然后换下一个数字，再下一个。三个周日就是156个周日。在前155个周日里，弗兰克·尼尔森·科尔都失败了。直到最后，在第156个周日，弗兰克·尼尔森·科尔找到了一个能整除它的数字，保罗说，这很正常。

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:23.020
<v Paul Hoffman>That's what science is about. It's real people banging their heads against walls and years of false starts. That's the other thing. We don't talk about the researcher who spent two years trying to find what this gene did and then gave up, or spent three years trying to find a planet outside the solar system and gave up, and someone else eventually did. It's more a combination of insight and hard work, because--
<v Paul Hoffman>这就是科学的意义所在。这是真实的人们在撞墙，经历了多年的失败尝试。还有一点。我们不谈论那些花了两年时间试图找出这个基因功能却最终放弃的研究人员，或者花了三年试图在太阳系外寻找行星却放弃了，而最终被别人做到了的情况。这更多是洞察力和努力工作的结合，因为——

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:23.530
<v Ira Glass>Failure.
<v Ira Glass>失败。

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:45.000
<v Paul Hoffman>And failure. Because people who think outside the box and achieve things outside the box often entertain a lot of wacky ideas that don't turn out to be true in the science world. Isaac Newton had lots of weird ideas. Charles Darwin had weird ideas. They happened to be right about what they're known for. Isaac Newton got a little interested in ghosts and all sorts of occult stuff.
<v Paul Hoffman>还有失败。因为那些跳出框架思考并取得框架外成就的人，往往会有很多古怪的想法，在科学界证明是不真实的。艾萨克·牛顿有很多奇怪的想法。查尔斯·达尔文也有奇怪的想法。他们刚好在他们出名的领域是对的。艾萨克·牛顿对鬼魂和各种神秘学的东西产生了一些兴趣。

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:45.790
<v Ira Glass>Really?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:46.240
<v Paul Hoffman>Yeah.
<v Paul Hoffman>是的。

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.150
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. And did he believe in ghosts, or did he just think, we gotta investigate this. Maybe there's some data here.
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。他相信鬼魂吗，还是他只是想，我们必须调查一下。也许这里有一些数据。

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.240
<v Paul Hoffman>No, he started believing in them. He--
<v Paul Hoffman>不，他开始相信它们了。他——

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:53.540
<v Ira Glass>Isaac Newton?
<v Ira Glass>艾萨克·牛顿？

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:54.450
<v Paul Hoffman>Yeah.
<v Paul Hoffman>是的。

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:55.080
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:06.750
<v Paul Hoffman>So they're known for their out-of-the-box thinking on something that turns out to be true and is wonderful. But they've also entertained a lot of other stuff.
<v Paul Hoffman>所以他们以在某些方面跳出框架的思考而闻名，这些思考最终被证明是正确的，而且是美妙的。但他们也涉足过很多其他事情。

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:48.940
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio program, we have two stories of people who face impossible-seeming situations. And in each of those stories, it's like a bad action film. These two people each decide that the stakes are high enough that their best shot is to try something crazy, so crazy it just might work. The first story is about a scientist. The second story is about somebody employing this technique in his personal life, which is really something to see. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I am hoping that all this sounds so crazy you just might stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们有两个关于人们面临看似不可能的情况的故事。在每一个故事中，就像一部糟糕的动作片。这两个人各自决定赌注足够大，以至于他们最好的赌注是尝试一些疯狂的事情，疯狂到可能真的会奏效。第一个故事是关于一位科学家的。第二个故事是关于某人在他的个人生活中运用这种技术，这真的很值得一看。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我希望所有这一切听起来如此疯狂，以至于你可能会留下来听我们的节目。

00:06:48.940 --> 00:07:16.940
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Mr. Holland's Opus. OK, true story. A guy goes to college on a music scholarship. And then afterwards, he ends up going into science. And he becomes a cancer researcher. His name is Jon Brody. And 19 years after graduating, Jon is invited back to his old college to give a talk about his work. And the speech he gives is mainly about how important it's been in his research to think outside the box, to use an overused phrase. To think outside the box, to be ready to turn away from what's familiar and try some new idea.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，霍兰先生的乐章。好的，真实的故事。一个家伙靠音乐奖学金上了大学。然后后来，他最终进入了科学领域。他成为了一名癌症研究员。他的名字叫乔恩·布罗迪。毕业19年后，乔恩被邀请回到他的老大学，谈谈他的工作。他发表的演讲主要是关于在他的研究中，跳出框架思考——用一个过度使用的短语——是多么重要。跳出框架思考，准备好摒弃熟悉的做法，尝试一些新想法。

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:40.070
<v Ira Glass>And then after his speech, Jon is approached by his old orchestra teacher, a guy named Anthony Holland. And Professor Holland, to Jon's great surprise, says, "Speaking of thinking outside the box, I've actually been working on an experiment for a few years that I'd like to show you. Come look at this video. I think you'll find it very interesting." So what could Jon do? He'd just actually given a whole talk about keeping an open mind.
<v Ira Glass>在演讲之后，乔恩的老管弦乐老师安东尼·霍兰走近了他。霍兰教授让乔恩大吃一惊，他说：“说到跳出框架思考，我其实已经做了一个实验几年了，我想展示给你看。来看看这个视频。我想你会觉得它非常有趣。”乔恩能做什么呢？他刚刚才发表了关于保持开放思想的整个演讲。

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:57.690
<v Ira Glass>What resulted from this was the kind of scientific collaboration that almost never, ever happens-- a serious cancer researcher teaming up with an amateur to try to make a breakthrough. Gabriel Rhodes is a documentary filmmaker. And he's been following the story from the beginning, back when Jon watched that video. Here's Gabriel.
<v Ira Glass>由此产生的是那种几乎永远不会发生的科学合作——一位严肃的癌症研究员与一位业余爱好者联手试图取得突破。加布里埃尔·罗兹是一位纪录片制片人。他从一开始就一直关注这个故事，回到乔恩观看那个视频的时候。这是加布里埃尔。

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:14.880
<v Gabriel Rhodes>I've seen the video that Anthony, the orchestra teacher, showed Jon. And it amazed me. I saw single-cell organisms, basically round and oblong and pear-shaped blobs, swimming around under a microscope. Anthony narrates this as he goes along. And suddenly, the cells stop and just self-destruct.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>我看过安东尼，那位管弦乐老师，展示给乔恩看的视频。这让我感到惊讶。我看到单细胞生物，基本上是圆形、长方形和梨形的斑点，在显微镜下游来游去。安东尼一边演示一边讲解。突然，细胞停止运动并自我毁灭。

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:20.800
<v Anthony Holland>Look what happened, just now. He suddenly and dramatically disintegrated--
<v Anthony Holland>看看刚才发生了什么。他突然戏剧性地瓦解了——

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:49.330
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Some burst into tiny fragments. And some look like a plastic bag that's been punctured, and their contents leak out. As Anthony explained to Jon, the theory behind what he was doing was simple. You know how a singer, if they hit the right frequency, can shatter a crystal glass? Well, Anthony was basically doing the same thing, directing specific frequencies at microorganisms, like bacteria and protozoa, to try and shatter them. And after a year and a half of experimentation, suddenly, it worked.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>有些分裂成微小的碎片。有些看起来像一个被刺破的塑料袋，里面的内容物漏了出来。正如安东尼向乔恩解释的那样，他所做工作的理论很简单。你知道歌手如果击中正确的频率，可以震碎水晶杯吗？好吧，安东尼基本上是在做同样的事情，将特定的频率导向微生物，如细菌和原生动物，试图震碎它们。经过一年半的实验，突然，它奏效了。

00:08:49.330 --> 00:09:00.840
<v Gabriel Rhodes>So Anthony showed this to Jon. And he and Jon each remember the conversation that followed a little differently, in a way that says a lot about their later collaboration. Anthony's version was entirely optimistic.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>所以安东尼向乔恩展示了这一点。他和乔恩对随后的谈话记忆略有不同，这在很大程度上说明了他们后来的合作。安东尼的版本完全是乐观的。

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:09.200
<v Anthony Holland>He immediately jumped right to the main thing that I was interested in. And he just said, "Do you think you can shatter cancer cells like that?"
<v Anthony Holland>他立刻跳到了我感兴趣的主要内容。他只是说：“你认为你能像那样震碎癌细胞吗？”

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:10.390
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And here's Jon.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>这是乔恩。

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:42.930
<v Jonathan Brody>You know, when I first heard about it, it's in the arena of UFOs, for sure. He was putting words in my mouth already. He was saying things like, "Do you think this would work?" And I would say, "Well, it's a possibility, and I'm willing to try anything." And so Anthony would, in his voice, he would would say, "So you're saying that I could maybe cure cancer." And I was like, "Well, so could the oboe player downstairs."
<v Jonathan Brody>你知道，当我第一次听说它时，它肯定处于UFO的领域。他已经在替我说话了。他在说像“你认为这会奏效吗？”之类的话。我会说，“嗯，这是一种可能性，我愿意尝试任何事情。”所以安东尼会用他的声音说，“所以你是说我可能可以治愈癌症。”我就像，“嗯，楼下的双簧管演奏者也可以。”

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:58.410
<v Gabriel Rhodes>But Jon was willing to talk more, in part, because he's so frustrated with cancer research. He thinks it's stalled. Lots of people going over the same familiar ideas, endlessly refining the same concepts, because the familiar is more likely to get funded.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>但乔恩愿意多谈谈，部分原因是他在癌症研究方面感到非常沮丧。他认为研究停滞不前。很多人在重复同样熟悉的概念，无休止地细化同样的概念，因为熟悉的事物更容易获得资助。

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:23.340
<v Jonathan Brody>I do think it's much like being out in Hollywood, where you might go out to Hollywood thinking that you're going to be Kafka. And you might end up writing the worst, hackiest sitcom in the world, because you want to survive. So what happens in this country when the same thing happens with science?
<v Jonathan Brody>我确实认为这很像是在好莱坞，你可能怀着成为卡夫卡的梦想去好莱坞。结果你可能最终写出了世界上最糟糕、最平庸的情景喜剧，因为你想生存。那么在这个国家，当科学发生同样的事情时会发生什么呢？

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:38.710
<v Gabriel Rhodes>I've known Jon Brody for 20 years. He's a good friend of mine. And before this encounter, Anthony was just a professor who drove him really hard, back when he went to college on a percussion scholarship. They weren't close or anything. But Jon definitely remembered Professor Holland as he'd always known him.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>我认识乔恩·布罗迪20年了。他是我的好朋友。在这次邂逅之前，安东尼只是一个在他大学期间对他要求非常严格的教授，当时他靠打击乐奖学金上大学。他们并不亲近。但乔恩绝对记得霍兰教授一直以来的样子。

00:10:38.710 --> 00:11:14.260
<v Jonathan Brody>He probably wouldn't say this, but I sort of remember him as-- I think he was disappointed in me as a musician in the orchestra, because I remember him wanting to have, like, extra sessions with me. And I think when we had a guest composer come in, I hate to say it, I think he relegated me to playing the triangle. And I'm not kidding. I seriously remember having to count, like, 67 bars to hit that one quarter note on the triangle.
<v Jonathan Brody>他可能不会这么说，但我记得他——我想他在管弦乐队里对我作为音乐家感到失望，因为我记得他想要我和他进行额外的辅导。而且我想当有客座作曲家来的时候，我不愿说，我想他把我降级去打三角铁了。我没开玩笑。我认真记得不得不数67小节才能在三角铁上敲那一个四分音符。

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:38.130
<v Gabriel Rhodes>So when Anthony came up to him after his speech, Jon was flattered that this teacher he'd always respected was interested in his work. They started emailing back and forth about Anthony's video and his experiments, until finally, Jon decided that he couldn't tell whether Anthony had something serious that could be researched unless he drove up to see him. So he went. And he invited me along just as a fun road trip. And why not film the conversation while I'm there?
<v Gabriel Rhodes>所以当安东尼在他的演讲后走到他身边时，乔恩感到受宠若惊，因为这位他一直尊敬的老师对他自己的工作感兴趣。他们开始来回发邮件讨论安东尼的视频和他的实验，直到最后，乔恩决定，除非他开车去见他，否则他无法判断安东尼是否有值得研究的严肃东西。所以他去了。他还邀请我一起去，就当是一次有趣的公路旅行。为什么不在我在那里的时候把谈话拍下来呢？

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.860
<v Anthony Holland>Is there enough light? I'm worried about the light.
<v Anthony Holland>光线足够吗？我担心光线。

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:42.080
<v Jonathan Brody>Oh, yeah.
<v Jonathan Brody>哦，是的。

00:11:42.080 --> 00:12:03.590
<v Gabriel Rhodes>We're up as Skidmore College in Saratoga Springs, New York, where Anthony teaches. Anthony's in his early '50s, but he's got a boyish vibe. He bounces a little when he walks. Full head of brown hair, goatee, glasses, corduroys. Your basic professor. Jon's in his late '30s, balding but in that stylish, close-cropped way. He stays in shape, even though he works like a maniac.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>我们在纽约州萨拉托加斯普林斯的斯基德莫尔学院，那是安东尼教书的地方。安东尼50岁出头，但他有一种孩子气。他走路时会轻轻跳跃。浓密的棕色头发，山羊胡子，眼镜，灯芯绒裤子。你基本的教授形象。乔恩30多岁，秃顶，但以那种时髦的短发方式。他保持身材，尽管他工作得像个疯子。

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:33.210
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And over the course of their long conversation, Jon was surprised and impressed by how much Anthony knew, how much he'd read, how dogged he'd been in his experiments. And one thing in particular caught Jon's attention. In Anthony's experiments, particular frequencies seemed to target particular organisms and left others untouched, meaning there might be frequencies that could destroy cancer cells while leaving normal, healthy cells alone-- a potential solution, Jon said, to the biggest problem with most cancer treatments.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>在他们长时间的交谈中，乔恩对安东尼了解多少、读过多少、在实验中多么顽强感到惊讶和钦佩。有一点特别引起了乔恩的注意。在安东尼的实验中，特定的频率似乎针对特定的生物，而让其他生物不受影响，这意味着可能存在一些频率可以破坏癌细胞，同时让正常的、健康的细胞不受影响——乔恩说，这可能是解决大多数癌症治疗最大问题的一个潜在方案。

00:12:33.210 --> 00:13:09.430
<v Jonathan Brody>So if we can find a frequency specific to cancer cells, we could put someone in a room, and you hit that frequency, and it's not affecting normal cells. I mean, that's the actual perfect paradigm in which to treat someone for this disease. Because what kills a patient is not the primary tumor. It's the tumors that have left the site and gone off into neighborhoods that the surgeon can't take out. So if you could walk into a room and you can get completely zapped, like a complete CAT scan--
<v Jonathan Brody>所以如果我们能找到一种针对癌细胞的频率，我们就可以把人放在一个房间里，当你击中那个频率时，它不会影响正常细胞。我的意思是，这实际上是治疗这种疾病的完美范式。因为杀死病人的不是原发肿瘤。而是那些已经离开原发部位、进入外科医生无法切除的邻近区域的肿瘤。所以如果你能走进一个房间，你能完全被扫描，就像一个完整的CAT扫描——

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:14.370
<v Anthony Holland>Right. And it only hits the cancer. It doesn't hurt healthy cells. Yeah, that would be the--
<v Anthony Holland>对。而且它只击中癌症。它不会伤害健康细胞。是的，那将是——

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:15.000
<v Jonathan Brody>Home run.
<v Jonathan Brody>本垒打。

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:15.880
<v Anthony Holland>That's the dream.
<v Anthony Holland>那是梦想。

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:21.250
<v Jonathan Brody>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Brody>是的。

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:38.850
<v Gabriel Rhodes>That's the dream, because chemotherapy and radiation are so flawed. Both devastate healthy cells. Anthony's machine works by pulsing the cells with electromagnetic waves at specific frequencies. And there are other scientists out there - published in peer-reviewed journals - who are also using electromagnetic waves to attack cancer cells. There's an FDA clinical trial going on right now using this method.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>那是梦想，因为化疗和放疗有太多的缺陷。两者都会破坏健康细胞。安东尼的机器通过以特定频率对细胞脉冲电磁波来工作。还有其他科学家——在同行评审期刊上发表文章——也正在使用电磁波攻击癌细胞。现在有一个使用这种方法的FDA临床试验正在进行中。

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:59.540
<v Gabriel Rhodes>The whole field is still very new, just a handful of people - and Anthony's system is different from others - but using electromagnetic waves to target cancer cells isn't just a fantasy dreamed up by a music professor. Then Jon asked Anthony how he'd gotten into these experiments in the first place.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>整个领域仍然非常新，只有少数人——而且安东尼的系统与其他人的不同——但使用电磁波来针对癌细胞不仅仅是音乐教授幻想出来的东西。然后乔恩问安东尼他是怎么开始这些实验的。

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:15.980
<v Anthony Holland>I read an interesting book called Lost Science . And I'm very interested in hidden information, in secret stuff we're not supposed to know and things maybe that were known a long time ago or forgotten or something.
<v Anthony Holland>我读过一本有趣的书叫《失落的科学》。我对隐藏的信息非常感兴趣，关于秘密的东西，我们不应该知道的事情，以及可能在很久以前就知道或者被遗忘了的东西。

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:53.990
<v Anthony Holland>And in that book-- now this is where it probably gets very kind of controversial-- but I read about a guy named Royal Raymond Rife. And the book says that in the 1930s, he built an electromagnetic frequency device, a radio frequency device, which cured cancer. I got the device. I developed a special, custom-built frequency synthesis program, because that's what I teach, digital audio synthesis. And I've been studying sound and physics and acoustics for a long time. And I began to run frequencies through this device.
<v Anthony Holland>在那本书里——现在这可能变得有点争议了——但我读到关于一个叫皇家雷蒙德·莱夫的人。书中说，在20世纪30年代，他制造了一种电磁频率装置，一种无线电频率装置，它治愈了癌症。我有这个装置。我开发了一个特殊的、定制的频率合成程序，因为那就是我教的内容，数字音频合成。我对声音、物理和声学研究了很长时间。我开始通过这个装置运行频率。

00:14:53.990 --> 00:15:23.140
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Now, Royal Rife, for lots of people who have even heard of him, is a name synonymous with bogus cancer cures. He was an inventor in the 1930s. And the legend is that he cured a bunch of people with cancer using an electromagnetic wave device before he was destroyed by a greedy, mainstream medical establishment. It's a staple of conspiracy theory websites. Google him. He's everywhere. If Jon wanted out of the box, Anthony and the Rife machine were way outside.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>现在，皇家莱夫，对于许多甚至听说过他的人来说，是一个与虚假癌症治疗同义的名字。他是20世纪30年代的一位发明家。传说他在被贪婪的主流医学界摧毁之前，用电磁波装置治愈了一群癌症患者。这是阴谋论网站的主打内容。去谷歌搜他。他无处不在。如果乔恩想跳出框架，安东尼和莱夫机器就远远超出了框架。

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:56.010
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And Jon and Anthony were just an unlikely pair. Jon was the head of his own lab by the age of 40. He gets hundreds of thousands of dollars from the National Institutes of Health and other very mainstream medical research organizations. Anthony, meanwhile, is a composer, electronics whiz, a self-taught expert in several fields, and a deep skeptic of all things mainstream, especially in medicine. He pulled me aside more than once to warn me I'd better be careful, my life might be in danger. Because big pharmaceutical companies would try to hush up any story about a possible cancer treatment alternative.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>乔恩和安东尼是一对不太可能的组合。乔恩40岁时就是他自己实验室的负责人了。他从国立卫生研究院和其他非常主流的医学研究组织获得数十万美元的资助。与此同时，安东尼是一位作曲家、电子奇才、多个领域的自学专家，也是所有主流事物的深度怀疑论者，特别是在医学领域。他不止一次把我拉到一边警告我要小心，我的生命可能有危险。因为大型制药公司会试图掩盖任何关于潜在癌症治疗替代方案的故事。

00:15:56.010 --> 00:16:04.930
<v Gabriel Rhodes>But they decided to work together. And it was just as much a personal decision as anything else. Each of them saw something in the other. They liked each other.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>但他们决定合作。这和任何其他事情一样是一个个人决定。他们每个人都在对方身上看到了某种东西。他们互相喜欢。

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:31.910
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And Jon thought, who cares where the electromagnetic wave theory came from? It's a potentially game-changing treatment. Why not at least test it? So he invited Anthony to come down to his lab in Philadelphia with the device. And they agreed to keep everything as low stakes as possible, half-serious, half a lark. Anthony would come for only two weeks. And they'd see what happened.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>乔恩想，电磁波理论从哪来有什么关系？这是一个潜在的改变游戏规则的治疗方法。为什么至少不测试一下呢？所以他邀请安东尼带着装置来到他在费城的实验室。他们同意把一切风险控制在最低限度，半认真，半开玩笑。安东尼只待两周。他们会看看发生什么。

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:37.390
<v Anthony Holland>This is the miracle amplifier, the secret weapon.
<v Anthony Holland>这是神奇的放大器，秘密武器。

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:57.310
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Anthony is in a room at Jon's lab at Thomas Jefferson University in Philadelphia, unpacking his equipment. And the plan for the next two weeks is that Anthony will work at night after everyone else leaves, so he isn't taking time or space away from other experiments. And he has to pay his own way. No tax dollars or grant money will be spent on this. Jon will supervise.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>安东尼在费城托马斯杰斐逊大学乔恩实验室的一个房间里，正在拆开他的设备。接下来的两周计划是安东尼在其他人离开后的晚上工作，这样他就不会占用其他人进行其他实验的时间或空间。而且他必须支付自己的费用。没有税款或补助金会花在这上面。乔恩会监督。

00:16:57.310 --> 00:17:21.050
<v Gabriel Rhodes>So here Anthony is, surrounded by amplifiers, cables, all sorts of equipment, none of which is in special cases or anything. It's all coming out of luggage, wrapped in bubble wrap, and into this room where they do molecular cloning. It's got microscopes, a big medical-grade incubator, a special sterile area called the Tissue Culture Hood. It's high-tech. Jon's watching Anthony assembling his gear.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>所以安东尼在这里，周围是放大器、电缆、各种设备，没有一个是放在特殊箱子里的。它们全都是从行李箱里拿出来的，用气泡膜包着，放到这个进行分子克隆的房间里。这里有显微镜，一个大的医疗级培养箱，一个特殊的无菌区域叫组织培养罩。这是高科技。乔恩看着安东尼组装他的装备。

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:23.410
<v Jonathan Brody>It looks like you literally made this in your garage.
<v Jonathan Brody>看起来你真的是在你的车库里做的。

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:41.960
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Jon said, "It looks like you literally made this in your garage." To me, the setup looks like it was designed by Dr. Seuss for some sci-fi movie in the '60s. There's some high-tech gear, like a big, glass, helium-filled tube with electrodes that glows pink when it's turned on, and then this whole mess of consumer electronics.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>乔恩说，“看起来你真的是在你的车库里做的。”对我来说，这个设置看起来像是苏斯博士为60年代的科幻电影设计的。有一些高科技装备，比如一个大的、充氦的玻璃管，有电极，打开时会发出粉红色的光，然后这一大堆消费电子产品。

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:56.690
<v Anthony Holland>This is a power supply that came from an electronics house in Cleveland. And then this little 100-watt magical amp I picked up from some company that sells CB radios to truckers or something on the internet.
<v Anthony Holland>这是一个从克利夫兰的一家电子公司买来的电源。然后这个小的100瓦神奇放大器是我从网上买的，卖给卡车司机CB电台之类的公司买的。

00:17:56.690 --> 00:18:05.190
<v Gabriel Rhodes>There's also two laptops. And on one, Anthony's written a program that generates frequencies to send into the device. It sounds like this--
<v Gabriel Rhodes>还有两台笔记本电脑。在一台笔记本上，安东尼写了一个程序，生成发送到装置的频率。它听起来像这样——

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:05.600
<v Gabriel Rhodes>[BEEP]
<v Gabriel Rhodes>[哔]

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:09.080
<v Anthony Holland>It's not a really fabulous sound. It's kind of ugly and annoying.
<v Anthony Holland>这并不是一个非常美妙的声音。它有点丑陋和烦人。

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:14.750
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Yeah. And then a transmitter transposes the frequencies way up out of the audible range.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>是的。然后一个发射器将频率转换到可听范围之外的高频。

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:34.650
<v Anthony Holland>And then, what we'll do is we'll set the cancer cells right here. And with any luck, if we get the right frequency, the cancer cells may in fact start dying.
<v Anthony Holland>然后，我们要做的就是把癌细胞放在这里。如果运气好，如果我们得到正确的频率，癌细胞事实上可能会开始死亡。

00:18:34.650 --> 00:19:02.630
<v Gabriel Rhodes>For the next two weeks, Anthony works all night, every night, running experiments, taking cancer cells that have been prepared for him out of the incubator. And he pulses the cells with frequencies for hours, trying one frequency after another. Jon checks in on the experiments every day. There's a lot of trial and error and problems. Some of the equipment blows out and has to be replaced. And Jon and Anthony aren't sure what they've got until Anthony's last day, when Jon comes to look at the latest experiments.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>接下来的两周，安东尼每天晚上都在工作，运行实验，从培养箱中取出已经为他准备好的癌细胞。他用频率对细胞脉冲数小时，尝试一个又一个频率。乔恩每天检查实验情况。有很多试验和错误以及问题。有些设备烧坏了，必须更换。乔恩和安东尼不确定他们得到了什么，直到安东尼的最后一天，当乔恩来看最新的实验。

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:05.320
<v Anthony Holland>Let's see. They're going to be labeled 720.
<v Anthony Holland>让我们看看。它们将被标记为720。

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:17.060
<v Gabriel Rhodes>They're sitting in front of a microscope, looking down at pancreatic cancer cells. The cells are in what looks like a tiny plastic egg carton. Jon looks down into the microscope, and he starts to get excited.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>他们坐在显微镜前，看着胰腺癌细胞。细胞在一个看起来像微小的塑料鸡蛋托盘里。乔恩向下看进显微镜，他开始感到兴奋。

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:18.340
<v Jonathan Brody>This is pretty surprising to me.
<v Jonathan Brody>这对我来说相当令人惊讶。

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:19.100
<v Anthony Holland>Really?
<v Anthony Holland>真的吗？

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:20.020
<v Jonathan Brody>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Brody>是的。

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:51.010
<v Gabriel Rhodes>A huge percentage of the pancreatic cells that Anthony pulsed with his device are dead. They combined the pulsing with a tiny bit of chemo. And of the cancer cells that Anthony pulsed, only 10% are still alive. A separate group of cells that he did not pulse has 60% alive. And this comparison is the key to the whole thing. In any experiment, you have the cells that you treat and the ones that you don't treat, which are called the control group. Comparing the treated cells to the control is the only way you can tell if something's working.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>安东尼用他的装置脉冲的大部分胰腺细胞都死了。他们将脉冲与一点点化疗结合起来。而在安东尼脉冲的癌细胞中，只有10%还活着。他没有脉冲的一组单独细胞有60%活着。这种比较是整个事情的关键。在任何实验中，你都有处理的细胞和不处理的细胞，这被称为对照组。将处理过的细胞与对照组进行比较是判断某事是否奏效的唯一方法。

00:19:51.010 --> 00:20:05.760
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Again, here, only 10% of the pulsed cells survived, versus 60% of the control cells. It's a dramatic result. And Jon gives everything to the lab tech Christina to count up the cells in a precise way, do the quantitative analysis.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>同样，在这里，只有10%的脉冲细胞存活下来，而对照组细胞存活了60%。这是一个巨大的结果。乔恩把一切都交给了实验室技术员克里斯蒂娜，以精确的方式计数细胞，进行定量分析。

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:10.030
<v Jonathan Brody>I mean, when she quantitates these, we're going to have a beautiful graph.
<v Jonathan Brody>我的意思是，当她定量这些时，我们将得到一个美丽的图表。

