<v Anthony Holland>Really?
<v Anthony Holland>真的吗？

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.840
<v Jonathan Brody>Yeah. I mean, a beautiful graph.
<v Jonathan Brody>是的。我的意思是，一个美丽的图表。

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:34.400
<v Gabriel Rhodes>A beautiful graph. That's code for Jon is thrilled. Pancreatic cancer is his specialty. And it's practically invincible, harder to kill than other cancers. So tough that even chemo, which is poison, often barely works at all. Over the last 25 years, the biggest treatment breakthrough only added about four months to a patient's life.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>一个美丽的图表。那是乔恩感到兴奋的暗语。胰腺癌是他的专业。而且它几乎是无敌的，比其他癌症更难杀死。太棘手了，以至于即使是化疗，也就是毒药，通常也几乎不起作用。在过去的25年里，最大的治疗突破只增加了病人大约四个月的寿命。

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:43.180
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Jon starts going through his mental checklist of potential problems with the experiment. This is what he does when he's excited about new results. And he checks with Anthony about the controls.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>乔恩开始检查他对实验潜在问题的心理清单。这就是当他对新结果感到兴奋时所做的。他向安东尼确认了对照组。

00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:49.530
<v Jonathan Brody>That's all you did to these things. And you took these out of the incubator, the others, out? The controls did you take out?
<v Jonathan Brody>你对这些东西只做了这些。你把这些从培养箱里拿出来了，其他的，拿出来了？对照组你拿出来了吗？

00:20:49.530 --> 00:21:00.090
<v Anthony Holland>No. The controls I left in the incubator. I know I probably should take them out for the six or seven hours that I'm treating them. But, um--
<v Anthony Holland>没有。对照组我留在了培养箱里。我知道我可能应该在治疗它们的六或七个小时里把它们拿出来。但是，嗯——

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:06.770
<v Jonathan Brody>Yeah, I thought you were doing that. That's why, that first day, I was asking you to do that.
<v Jonathan Brody>是的，我以为你在做那个。这就是为什么，第一天，我要求你这样做。

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:07.840
<v Anthony Holland>Right.
<v Anthony Holland>对。

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:26.180
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Jon's expression doesn't change much. But the experiment is now a wash. The cells that Anthony was pulsing sat outside the incubator for hours at a time while he was pulsing them. Therefore, the control plates also had to sit outside the incubator for the exact same amount of time. Otherwise, they don't count as controls.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>乔恩的表情没有太大变化。但实验现在作废了。安东尼正在脉冲的细胞在脉冲它们时在培养箱外放了几个小时。因此，对照盘也必须在培养箱外放相同的时间。否则，它们不能算作对照组。

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:47.970
<v Gabriel Rhodes>It was an amateur's mistake. And of course, Anthony is an amateur. But getting the controls exactly right turns out to be one of the hardest parts of any experiment. So even though Anthony's two weeks are up-- he has to leave today-- they've got to do the experiments again.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>这是一个业余的错误。当然，安东尼是一个业余爱好者。但要把对照组完全做好，是任何实验中最困难的部分之一。所以尽管安东尼的两周结束了——他今天必须离开——他们必须重新进行实验。

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:59.450
<v Gabriel Rhodes>The first chance Anthony has to go back to Jon's lab is six months later. It's a whole day's drive. He's on a sabbatical, so he can be here for five weeks this time. I visit him about three weeks in.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>安东尼回到乔恩实验室的第一次机会是六个月后。要开车一整天。他正在休学术假，所以这次他可以在这里待五周。我在他待了大约三周时去看他。

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:18.660
<v Anthony Holland>You ask me how it's going. It's like the Tale of Two Cities. It's going really great on one hand, and it's going really badly on the other hand. It's going really great because we have absolute proof-- and we'll see what Jon says later today when we see him. But we're blowing cancer cells away.
<v Anthony Holland>你问我进展如何。这就像《双城记》。一方面进展得非常好，另一方面进展得非常糟糕。进展得非常好是因为我们有绝对的证据——等今天晚些时候见到乔恩时，我们会看看他怎么说。但我们正在震碎癌细胞。

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:53.780
<v Gabriel Rhodes>This is how Anthony sees it, because night after night, he's watching cancer cells die and taking thousands of time-lapse photos and videos as it happens, like the video that he first showed Jon. The problem is, these photos are not absolute proof at all, not to any scientist, not to Jon. Cancer cells die for all kinds of reasons. Absolute proof means only one thing-- counting the cells in the experiments, the treated ones and the control groups, and comparing them. So while Anthony might think they have proof, Jon would never say that.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>这就是安东尼的看法，因为夜复一夜，他在看着癌细胞死亡，拍摄了数以千计的延时照片和视频，就像他第一次展示给乔恩看的那个视频一样。问题是，这些照片根本不是绝对的证据，对任何科学家，对乔恩来说都不是。癌细胞死亡有各种各样的原因。绝对证据只有一件事——计数实验中的细胞，处理过的和对照组的，然后进行比较。所以虽然安东尼可能认为他们有证据，乔恩永远不会那样说。

00:22:53.780 --> 00:23:13.950
<v Gabriel Rhodes>In these last three weeks, Anthony's had a series of setbacks. Basically, the photos are all he's got from a bunch of experiments that had to be thrown out. But finally, he starts to get incredible results. Jon gave him leukemia cells for the first time. And the pulsing seems to have killed 30% more of the cancer cells compared with the controls.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>在过去的三周里，安东尼经历了一系列的挫折。基本上，这些照片是他从一群必须被扔掉的实验中得到的全部。但最后，他开始得到令人难以置信的结果。乔恩第一次给了他白血病细胞。与对照组相比，脉冲似乎多杀死了30%的癌细胞。

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:17.970
<v Jonathan Brody>You're doing something to these. You're doing something here.
<v Jonathan Brody>你正在对这些做些什么。你在这里做了些什么。

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:20.250
<v Anthony Holland>Well, yeah, I have video.
<v Anthony Holland>好吧，是的，我有视频。

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:22.100
<v Jonathan Brody>We can get quantitation on this.
<v Jonathan Brody>我们可以从中得到定量数据。

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:27.430
<v Anthony Holland>Really? Hey, if we can get quantitative numbers out of this, I'll keep hammering these guys.
<v Anthony Holland>真的吗？嘿，如果我们能从中得到定量数字，我会继续敲打这些家伙。

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:38.020
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Jon turns to Anthony and starts going through his mental checklist. Again he's excited. He's thinking that maybe, if everything's right, these results are good enough to publish in a paper.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>乔恩转向安东尼，开始检查他的心理清单。他再次感到兴奋。他认为也许，如果一切正确，这些结果足以发表在一篇论文中。

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:51.570
<v Jonathan Brody>Now Anthony, I'm serious about this. You need to think about, for me, like anything I've done for you, you need to think about, is there any-- these plates just sit out here, and then you bring the other plate in there. And there's nothing different that you do to those plates.
<v Jonathan Brody>现在安东尼，我是认真的。你需要为我想一想，就像我为你做的一切一样，你需要考虑，有没有——这些盘子就放在这里，然后你把另一个盘子拿到那里。除了这些盘子，你没有做任何不同的事情。

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:52.850
<v Anthony Holland>Nope.
<v Anthony Holland>没有。

00:23:52.850 --> 00:23:54.440
<v Jonathan Brody>Except for the--
<v Jonathan Brody>除了——

00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:56.620
<v Anthony Holland>Just electronic pulsing.
<v Anthony Holland>只是电子脉冲。

00:23:56.620 --> 00:24:03.490
<v Jonathan Brody>Because if we publish this, your name will go first. My name will go last. It's our names on the line. We're married together.
<v Jonathan Brody>因为如果我们发表这个，你的名字会排在第一位。我的名字会排在最后。这是我们的名字在冒险。我们结婚在一起了。

00:24:03.490 --> 00:24:05.390
<v Anthony Holland>Yeah, I understand that.
<v Anthony Holland>是的，我理解。

00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:06.620
<v Jonathan Brody>So our reputation is married.
<v Jonathan Brody>所以我们的名誉是结婚的。

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:12.520
<v Anthony Holland>I think I should be a technical footnote at the end, "Thanks to Anthony Holland for assistance."
<v Anthony Holland>我认为我应该作为末尾的技术脚注，“感谢安东尼·霍兰的协助。”

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:16.460
<v Jonathan Brody>I think that's absurd.
<v Jonathan Brody>我认为那是荒谬的。

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:17.140
<v Anthony Holland>It wouldn't bother me.
<v Anthony Holland>我不会在意的。

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:22.010
<v Jonathan Brody>That's one of the most absurd comments you've made since I've known you. And you've made a few.
<v Jonathan Brody>这是我认识你以来说过最荒谬的评论之一。而且你已经说了不少了。

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:29.580
<v Anthony Holland>That's OK with me. I mean that.
<v Anthony Holland>我没关系。我是认真的。

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:30.460
<v Jonathan Brody>Careful here.
<v Jonathan Brody>小心点。

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:41.520
<v Gabriel Rhodes>A couple days later, I talk to Jon up on the roof of his apartment building. They're still waiting for the numbers, the analysis. But now, they've got both leukemia and pancreatic experiments that they're fired up about.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>几天后，我在他的公寓楼顶和乔恩谈话。他们仍在等待数字，分析结果。但现在，他们既有白血病实验，也有胰腺实验，他们对此非常激动。

00:24:41.520 --> 00:25:20.090
<v Jonathan Brody>I mean, they're astounding results and sort of unprecedented. There could be a lot of reasons behind it besides the pulsing. And that's why we need to reproduce it. What makes me nervous and anxious is if I'm truly the only cancer researcher using this apparatus since Royal Rife, because again, that puts even more pressure on me to publish something that's real. And of course, I want it to work. It's potentially something very exciting. But it's still very much up in the air.
<v Jonathan Brody>我的意思是，这些结果令人震惊，而且是史无前例的。除了脉冲之外，背后可能有很多原因。这就是为什么我们需要重现它。让我感到紧张和焦虑的是，如果我真的是皇家莱夫之后唯一使用这个装置的癌症研究员，因为那又给我带来了发布真实东西的压力。当然，我希望它奏效。它是一个潜在的非常令人兴奋的东西。但它仍然处于空中楼阁的状态。

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:22.820
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Anthony's in a very different place.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>安东尼处于一个非常不同的地方。

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:28.110
<v Anthony Holland>[SINGING] Somewhere over the rainbow.
<v Anthony Holland>[歌唱] 在彩虹之上某处。

00:25:28.110 --> 00:26:10.320
<v Anthony Holland>I kind of feel like that. We're living in Oz right now, because we're doing the impossible. What was Oz all about? Oz was all about, Dorothy goes to a wonderful place where miracles happen. That's where I am, because we're proving the impossible. We're proving that a Rife machine kills cancer. We're not proving. It's been proven. The question is, how much proof can we pile up now for other people? As Jon said, no one's going to believe it. Well, the rest of my life, no matter what anybody says, I know it works.
<v Anthony Holland>我感觉就像那样。我们现在住在奥兹国，因为我们正在做不可能的事情。奥兹国是关于什么的？奥兹国是关于，多萝西去了一个奇迹发生的美妙地方。这就是我所在的地方，因为我们正在证明不可能的事情。我们正在证明莱夫机器可以杀死癌症。我们不是在证明。它已经被证明了。问题是，我们现在可以为其他人堆积多少证据？正如乔恩所说，没有人会相信它。好吧，我的余生，不管别人说什么，我知道它奏效了。

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:37.420
<v Gabriel Rhodes>By the end of August, a year since they started this process, Anthony's done two rounds of experiments. And he's about to finish a final round. The plan is for Anthony to reproduce the great leukemia and pancreatic results one more time, with perfect controls, including a special kind of control called a sham. Jon has asked Anthony to start writing up a paper to submit for publication while he's finishing the experiments. And he's more confident about the project than I've ever seen him.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>到8月底，距离他们开始这个过程已经一年了，安东尼完成了两轮实验。他即将完成最后一轮。计划是安东尼再重现一次伟大的白血病和胰腺实验结果，带有完美的对照组，包括一种特殊的对照组，叫做伪实验。乔恩要求安东尼在完成实验的同时开始撰写一篇论文提交发表。他对这个项目比我见过的任何时候都更有信心。

00:26:37.420 --> 00:27:10.540
<v Jonathan Brody>Just to let you know where I'm at, I'm excited about finishing this paper, putting my name on the paper. I'm willing to put my name out there so much that I'll be the senior author, the last author. And I'm willing to send in an NIH grant-- I don't know if we'll get it together-- for October. So this will be a six-page, $250,000 grant to pursue this work with Anthony. And I believe it so much that we're starting to administratively think about it and move this forward.
<v Jonathan Brody>只是让你知道我的进展，我很兴奋能完成这篇论文，把我的名字写在论文上。我愿意把我的名字写在上面，我将成为资深作者，最后一位作者。而且我愿意提交一份NIH补助金——我不知道我们是否能在10月之前完成——所以这将是一份六页的、25万美元的补助金，用于与安东尼一起进行这项工作。我非常相信它，以至于我们开始在行政上考虑它，并推动它向前发展。

00:27:10.540 --> 00:27:55.590
<v Jonathan Brody>If these controls work out well, his therapy's working at this stage of the game. And I wouldn't have predicted that. I mean, I know I'm going to get criticism about it. I know they're like, what are you doing? What is this? Do you really believe this stuff? I'm very serious about my reputation. And some scientists don't have an open mind. I'm definitely nervous about that aspect.
<v Jonathan Brody>如果这些对照组结果很好，他的疗法在这个阶段就是奏效的。而我不会预测到这一点。我的意思是，我知道我会因此受到批评。我知道他们会说，你在做什么？这是什么？你真的相信这些东西吗？我对我的声誉非常认真。而且有些科学家没有开放的头脑。我绝对对此感到紧张。

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:57.820
<v Anthony Holland>Hello, Jonathan. How you doing?
<v Anthony Holland>你好，乔纳森。你好吗？

00:27:57.820 --> 00:27:59.700
<v Jonathan Brody>I'm doing OK. How are you?
<v Jonathan Brody>我很好。你呢？

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:01.110
<v Anthony Holland>Very good.
<v Anthony Holland>很好。

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:26.320
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And then, bad news. Really bad news. I'm up at Skidmore with Anthony when Jon calls with the data about the final round of experiments. At first, they talk about the Excel spreadsheet with data from the control experiment called a sham. There was a bit of a strange result there. But the real bad news comes when Jon asks Anthony to open up the PowerPoint presentation and look at the data from the pancreatic experiments.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>然后，坏消息。真的是坏消息。我在斯基德莫尔和安东尼在一起时，乔恩打电话来告知关于最后一轮实验的数据。起初，他们谈论的是来自被称为伪实验的对照实验数据的Excel电子表格。那里有一个有点奇怪的结果。但真正的坏消息是乔恩要求安东尼打开PowerPoint演示文稿，查看来自胰腺实验的数据。

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:35.790
<v Anthony Holland>PowerPoint. Let's see, room temperature-- whoa. What is this a PowerPoint of?
<v Anthony Holland>PowerPoint。让我们看看，室温——哇。这是什么PowerPoint？

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:48.030
<v Jonathan Brody>Well, the pulsing basically caused the cells to become resistant to the drugs. And they grew better.
<v Jonathan Brody>好吧，脉冲基本上导致细胞对药物产生了耐药性。它们长得更好。

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:53.710
<v Anthony Holland>This is really weird, because it's very opposite of what we saw in previous experiments.
<v Anthony Holland>这真的很奇怪，因为它与我们在之前实验中看到的完全相反。

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:55.950
<v Jonathan Brody>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Brody>是的。

00:28:55.950 --> 00:29:23.670
<v Gabriel Rhodes>It's a disaster. It's a huge setback. It's shocking. The data show that in the plate of treated pancreatic cells, the ones pulsed with electromagnetic waves and given a tiny dose of chemo, 100% of the cancer cells were alive after treatment. Meaning not only did the pulsing not kill any cancer cells, but somehow, the pulsing seemed to protect the cancer cells from being killed by the chemo. Anthony is stunned. He keeps saying the same thing again and again.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>这是一场灾难。这是一个巨大的挫折。这令人震惊。数据显示，在处理过的胰腺细胞盘中，那些用电磁波脉冲并给予少量化疗的细胞，100%的癌细胞在治疗后仍然活着。这意味着脉冲不仅没有杀死任何癌细胞，而且不知何故，脉冲似乎保护了癌细胞免受化疗的杀伤。安东尼惊呆了。他一次又一次地重复同样的话。

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:30.680
<v Anthony Holland>All the other experiments show completely different results. I don't understand that. It doesn't make any sense to me.
<v Anthony Holland>所有其他实验显示的结果完全不同。我不明白。这对我来说根本讲不通。

00:29:30.680 --> 00:29:32.550
<v Jonathan Brody>It's a slap in the face, isn't it?
<v Jonathan Brody>这是一个耳光，不是吗？

00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:34.290
<v Anthony Holland>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Anthony Holland>是的。是的。

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:45.830
<v Jonathan Brody>Yeah. So I'll go through stepwise, OK? Because there's some things we definitely need to discuss here.
<v Jonathan Brody>是的。所以我将分步骤进行，好吗？因为这里有一些我们绝对需要讨论的事情。

00:29:45.830 --> 00:30:17.290
<v Gabriel Rhodes>They talk for 45 minutes, going over every aspect of the experiment. Anthony had used a different microscope this time. Maybe that was a problem. The microscope light that Anthony used when he took time-lapsed photos of the experiments, was the light heating the cells? And the amp for the device had been outputting less power than usual. Maybe some of the components were damaged. But the upshot of these last experiments is different for Jon and Anthony. Anthony sees these results as a fluke, an outlier. The other results still seem valid to him.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>他们谈了45分钟，逐一检查实验的各个方面。安东尼这次使用了不同的显微镜。也许那是一个问题。安东尼在拍摄实验延时照片时使用的显微镜灯，灯光是否在加热细胞？而且该装置的放大器输出的功率比平时少。也许有些组件损坏了。但这些最后实验的结果对于乔恩和安东尼来说是不同的。安东尼认为这些结果是侥幸，是一个离群点。其他结果对他来说仍然有效。

00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:46.530
<v Jonathan Brody>But for Jon, there's only one truth. If they want to publish, they've got to do more experiments. Anthony has to reproduce and confirm those great earlier results with controls. For me, doing this for 20-some-odd years or whatever it is, it doesn't surprise me that it's going to be this complicated. But I'm in it for the long haul, as far as the collaboration is, to try to figure this out. And we'll just keep at it, OK?
<v Jonathan Brody>但对乔恩来说，只有一个真相。如果他们想发表，他们必须做更多的实验。安东尼必须用对照组重现并确认那些伟大的早期结果。对我来说，做了20多年左右的这个，对于它会如此复杂并不感到惊讶。但就合作而言，我对此有长期的打算，试图弄清楚这一点。我们会继续努力，好吗？

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:50.850
<v Anthony Holland>OK.
<v Anthony Holland>好的。

00:30:50.850 --> 00:31:06.660
<v Gabriel Rhodes>But that wasn't what happened. In fact, for an entire year, Jon wasn't sure what was going on. And neither was I. He offered to go up to Skidmore to set up a lab for Anthony there if he couldn't come back down to Philadelphia. But that didn't happen. And then Anthony became very hard to reach.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>但那并不是发生的。事实上，整整一年，乔恩都不知道发生了什么。我也不知道。如果安东尼不能回到费城，他提出去斯基德莫尔为安东尼建立一个实验室。但这并没有发生。然后安东尼变得非常难以联系。

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:27.080
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And a couple of weeks after Jon talked to Anthony by phone for the first time in several months, I go and see Jon. He's decided to go up to Saratoga the following week to talk to Anthony face to face about the experiment. In the last year, Jon's become more of a skeptic, partly about the device itself, but also about the collaboration with Anthony. Why would Anthony just drop out like this?
<v Gabriel Rhodes>在乔恩几个月来第一次通过电话与安东尼谈话后的几周，我去看乔恩。他决定下周去萨拉托加面对面地与安东尼谈论这个实验。在过去的一年里，乔恩变得更加怀疑，部分是对装置本身的怀疑，但也对与安东尼的合作产生了怀疑。为什么安东尼会这样退出？

00:31:27.080 --> 00:32:15.620
<v Jonathan Brody>If he really believes in it and he really believes those results, let's do it again. If you cured cancer cells, let's do it again. My lab is open. My lab is open. My expertise is open. I'm happy-- you don't want to do it in Philadelphia? Let's do it in Saratoga. Ask him, why hasn't he taken me up on that? Let me ask you a question. If you knew you had something that killed cancer cells, and you believed in it, you really believed in it, you could wait a year to try to prove that because one set of experiments didn't work? I mean, come on.
<v Jonathan Brody>如果他真的相信它，并且他真的相信那些结果，让我们再做一次。如果你治愈了癌细胞，让我们再做一次。我的实验室是开放的。我的实验室是开放的。我的专业知识是开放的。我很高兴——你不想在费城做吗？让我们在萨拉托加做。问问他，为什么他没有接受我的提议？让我问你一个问题。如果你知道你有什么东西可以杀死癌细胞，并且你相信它，你真的相信它，你能等一年试图证明这一点，仅仅因为一组实验没有奏效？我的意思是，拜托。

00:32:15.620 --> 00:32:36.220
<v Gabriel Rhodes>This is one of the big differences between Jon and Anthony, between scientist and non-scientist. For Jon, having a year's worth of work suddenly thrown into question is a normal day at the office. But for Anthony, that's not normal. And it's not OK. The time in Jon's lab was a year of his life, where he felt like Jon kept moving the goal posts.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>这是乔恩和安东尼，科学家和非科学家之间最大的区别之一。对于乔恩来说，一年的工作突然受到质疑是办公室里的正常一天。但对于安东尼来说，那是不正常的。那是不可以的。在乔恩实验室的时间是他生命中的一年，他觉得乔恩一直在移动球门线。

00:32:36.220 --> 00:33:01.210
<v Gabriel Rhodes>I felt that way, too, sometimes as I was watching. Jon kept saying he was amazed at what he was seeing and just about convinced. But then he'd wake up in the middle of the night with another experiment or control that he wanted to run. And there was no telling when it would end, which for him, again, is normal. But now, Anthony wants to know, before he starts turning his life upside down again, what will count as proof enough for Jon? How many experiments?
<v Gabriel Rhodes>我在观察时，有时也有那种感觉。乔恩一直说他对所看到的感到惊讶，几乎被说服了。但随后他会在半夜醒来，想进行另一个实验或对照实验。而且没人知道什么时候结束，对他来说，再次强调，那是正常的。但现在，安东尼想知道，在他再次把自己的生活颠倒之前，什么对乔恩来说算得上足够的证明？多少实验？

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:26.210
<v Anthony Holland>So let's say I do three weeks of experiment, and I only concentrate on these leukemia cells. And if I can kill at least 20% every single time, every week, will that do it? Would that be enough? Or do you want to see pancreatic die, or do you want to see-- I mean, what exact buttons do I have to hit?
<v Anthony Holland>所以假设我做三周的实验，我只专注于这些白血病细胞。如果我能每次，每周都至少杀死20%，那能行吗？那足够了吗？或者你想看到胰腺细胞死亡，或者你想看到——我的意思是，我到底必须按什么按钮？

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:39.550
<v Gabriel Rhodes>When Jon gets to Saratoga, he and Anthony embrace and smile. And they're clearly glad to see each other. But as soon as they start talking about the experiments, they start arguing. And they don't understand each other. Jon keeps coming back to the same point.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>当乔恩到达萨拉托加时，他和安东尼拥抱并微笑。他们显然很高兴见到对方。但一旦他们开始谈论实验，他们就开始争论。他们互相不理解。乔恩不断回到同一点。

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:48.830
<v Jonathan Brody>Why can't you accept that we haven't proven anything? In the court of scientific law, we haven't proven anything.
<v Jonathan Brody>为什么你不能接受我们什么也没证明的事实？在科学法律的法庭上，我们什么也没证明。

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:51.430
<v Anthony Holland>But we have very intriguing results.
<v Anthony Holland>但我们有非常有趣的结果。

00:33:51.430 --> 00:34:26.060
<v Jonathan Brody>That's fine. I mean, if you want to leave it at that, you could say that. But there's a big difference between intriguing and promising results and actually showing and demonstrating that something works. There's a huge difference. So 10 years from now, would you still walk around campus and be like, you know what? I killed those cancer cells. I actually did that. I actually proved that this worked. Do you think, deep down, that's the way you feel? That's what I want to know.
<v Jonathan Brody>没关系。我的意思是，如果你想停留在那里，你可以这么说。但有趣和有希望的结果与实际展示和证明某事是有效之间有很大的区别。有一个巨大的区别。所以10年后，你还会走在校园里，然后说，你知道吗？我杀死了那些癌细胞。我真的做到了。我真的证明了这一点奏效。你认为，在内心深处，你是那样感觉的吗？这就是我想知道的。

00:34:26.060 --> 00:34:36.490
<v Anthony Holland>We're not just talking about the last experiment, right? We're talking about the whole thing. In general? Are you kidding? I would frame this chart showing 43% of the leukemia cells dead. And I would say, we nailed them.
<v Anthony Holland>我们不仅仅是在谈论最后一个实验，对吧？我们是在谈论整个事情。总的来说？你在开玩笑吗？我会把这个显示43%的白血病细胞死亡的图表裱起来。我会说，我们搞定了它们。

00:34:36.490 --> 00:34:44.900
<v Jonathan Brody>Let me ask you a question. Let me ask you a question. If you're sitting on the cure for cancer, why aren't we doing the experiments?
<v Jonathan Brody>让我问你一个问题。让我问你一个问题。如果你正坐拥癌症的治愈方法，为什么我们不做实验？

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:46.950
<v Anthony Holland>I can't afford it.
<v Anthony Holland>我负担不起。

00:34:46.950 --> 00:34:53.980
<v Jonathan Brody>So you're telling me, you're willing to say to the world that you think you have the cure for cancer, but you can't afford it.
<v Jonathan Brody>所以你告诉我，你愿意对世界说你认为你有癌症的治愈方法，但你负担不起。

00:34:53.980 --> 00:34:56.070
<v Anthony Holland>Yeah.
<v Anthony Holland>是的。

00:34:56.070 --> 00:35:20.770
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Anthony says he's run out of money. He spent six years, including the time he worked on his own with protozoa, putting his own money into these experiments. Equipment, travel, living expenses while he was at Jon's lab, many, many thousands of dollars. He won't say exactly how much. And then, after he got back from the last round of experiments at Jon's lab, it was like he was looking up from the microscope at his life for the first time in a while.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>安东尼说他的钱花光了。他花了六年时间，包括他自己研究原生动物的时间，把自己的钱投进了这些实验。设备、旅行、他在乔恩实验室时的生活费用，成千上万的美元。他不会确切地说多少。然后，在他从乔恩实验室的最后一轮实验回来后，就像他有一段时间第一次从显微镜中抬头看他的生活一样。

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:47.090
<v Anthony Holland>I tell you, I came home from that year, and I just look at the house, and I just see, oh my god, I lost track of what's going on here. We need a new roof on the house. And we've got a huge leak in the bathroom. The plumbing's all screwed up. And I've been making too many sacrifices.
<v Anthony Holland>我告诉你，我从那一年回到家，我就看看房子，我就看到，天哪，我失去了对这里正在发生的事情的追踪。我们需要给房子换个新屋顶。而且浴室里有个巨大的漏水。管道系统全都搞砸了。我做出了太多的牺牲。

00:35:47.090 --> 00:36:01.330
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Jon doesn't offer to fund another round of experiments. He's got a tight budget. And at this point, like I said, he's not feeling so hopeful about Anthony and his experiment. He's more optimistic about the 30-plus other experiments he's got moving ahead in his lab.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>乔恩没有提议资助另一轮实验。他的预算很紧。而在这一点上，就像我说的，他对安东尼和他的实验感觉没那么有希望。他对他在实验室里正在进行的30多个其他实验更为乐观。

00:36:01.330 --> 00:36:13.960
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And Anthony refuses to ask for money to proceed. He says that would be rude. But he says he may have a different solution to the money problem. He starts showing Jon his new website for his new nonprofit company, called--
<v Gabriel Rhodes>而安东尼拒绝要求金钱继续。他说那很不礼貌。但他确实说他可能有另一个解决资金问题的办法。他开始给乔恩看他的新非营利公司的网站，叫——

00:36:13.960 --> 00:36:21.390
<v Anthony Holland>Novobiotronics Incorporated, "the future of biotechnology research," and a lot of dramatic statements.
<v Anthony Holland>Novobiotronics Incorporated，“生物技术研究的未来”，以及许多戏剧性的声明。

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:46.050
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Apparently, for the past year, Anthony's been forming this nonprofit company and building the website, which has tabs for all the different research he hopes to do with the device. Not just cancer, but also Lyme disease, malaria. He doesn't get into the malaria or any part of that with Jon. But he does show him the pages and pages where he explains, in detail, the cancer experiments they've done, without mentioning Jon's name or the name of his lab, since he doesn't have permission.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>显然，在过去的一年里，安东尼一直在组建这家非营利公司并建立网站，该网站有他希望用该装置进行的所有不同研究的标签。不仅仅是癌症，还有莱姆病、疟疾。他没有和乔恩深入探讨疟疾或那方面的任何部分。但他确实向他展示了页面和页面，他详细解释了他们所做的癌症实验，没有提到乔恩的名字或他的实验室的名字，因为他没有得到许可。

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:57.960
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Anthony says his plan is to use the website to raise money so they can continue the experiments. I thought Jon might be angry. Why hadn't Anthony told him that this is what he'd been doing all year? But Jon's reaction is--
<v Gabriel Rhodes>安东尼说他的计划是利用网站筹集资金，以便他们能继续实验。我以为乔恩可能会生气。为什么安东尼没有告诉他这一年来他一直在做这些事？但乔恩的反应是——

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:10.530
<v Jonathan Brody>This is amazing. I wish that all the students or the graduate students or people or the scientists that believed in what they were doing took this sort of initiative.
<v Jonathan Brody>这太棒了。我希望所有相信自己所做事情的学生、研究生或人们或科学家都能采取这种主动。

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:12.770
<v Anthony Holland>Wow. That's nice to hear.
<v Anthony Holland>哇。听到这个很高兴。

00:37:12.770 --> 00:37:34.240
<v Jonathan Brody>I think it's a beautiful website, Anthony. I think it's great. I think my only concern is, again, which is my overall concern of where things are at, is you have to be really careful here, that, really, a trained eye or a trained scientist is going to immediately question the overabundance of data without the controls and without the reproducibility.
<v Jonathan Brody>我认为这是一个美丽的网站，安东尼。我认为这很棒。我想我唯一的担忧是，再次强调，我对事情进展的整体担忧是，你在这里必须非常小心，真的，训练有素的眼睛或训练有素的科学家会立即质疑在没有对照组和没有重现性的情况下数据的过剩。

00:37:34.240 --> 00:38:10.460
<v Anthony Holland>So let me respond to this idea that intelligent and expert trained scientists, if shown this data, might dismiss it for lack of more information on the controls. And here's where I've been saving the best information for last, for a sort of dramatic and controversial revelation. I've had three important scientists in the field, in the field of cancer research, contact me about my work. They have seen this data. They have seen all of our data.
<v Anthony Holland>所以让我回应这个想法，即如果展示这些数据，聪明和专业的训练有素的科学家可能会因为对照组缺乏更多信息而驳回它。在这里，我把最好的信息留到了最后，作为一种戏剧性和有争议的启示。我有三位该领域的重要科学家，在癌症研究领域，联系我谈论我的工作。他们看到了这些数据。他们看到了我们所有的数据。

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:15.200
<v Gabriel Rhodes>This changes the conversation entirely. Suddenly, Jon's reputation is on the line.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>这完全改变了谈话。突然，乔恩的名誉岌岌可危。

00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:31.910
<v Jonathan Brody>I would never give that data out to another scientist. It wasn't controlled. That's highly inappropriate behavior that you did, that you actually went behind my back and you sent this to other researchers, where I allowed you to use my credibility and my lab to do this work.
<v Jonathan Brody>我永远不会把这些数据交给另一位科学家。它没有对照。那是你做的非常不恰当的行为，你实际上背着我把这个发给了其他研究人员，而我允许你利用我的信誉和我的实验室来做这项工作。

00:38:31.910 --> 00:38:41.540
<v Anthony Holland>OK, so here's my response. First of all, if there was some breach in protocol I made by sharing some preliminary data--
<v Anthony Holland>好吧，这是我的回应。首先，如果我通过分享一些初步数据在协议上有一些违规——

00:38:41.540 --> 00:38:48.220
<v Jonathan Brody>Why didn't you ask me? Why didn't you say, "Look, someone's contacting me. Do you mind if I share some of our data together?"
<v Jonathan Brody>你为什么不问我？你为什么不说，“看，有人在联系我。你不介意我分享我们一起的一些数据吗？”

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:49.710
<v Anthony Holland>Could I finish without being interrupted?
<v Anthony Holland>我能不被打断地完成吗？

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:50.720
<v Jonathan Brody>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Brody>是的。

00:38:50.720 --> 00:39:01.300
<v Anthony Holland>If I broke some protocol, I apologize. You never told me, "Keep this data secret and don't show it to anybody." It just was given freely--
<v Anthony Holland>如果我打破了一些协议，我道歉。你从来没有告诉过我，“把这些数据保密，不要展示给任何人。”它只是被自由地给予了——

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:13.310
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Even though Anthony promises he didn't mention Jon's name to these other scientists, if one of those scientists were to do a quick Google search, in a few clicks, he could get to Jon, who would then be associated with data he would never want public.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>即使安东尼承诺他没有向其他科学家提到乔恩的名字，如果其中一位科学家快速搜索谷歌，点击几下，他就能找到乔恩，然后他会与他永远不想公开的数据联系在一起。

00:39:13.310 --> 00:39:33.810
<v Anthony Holland>I'm sorry. I mean, to me as a composer, it's like if some violinist says, "Hey, do you have a piece for a violin?" I say, "Well, I have a sketch. It's nothing really great, but I have a little start." "Well, let me see what it is." "Sure. I'll send it along." So it's the same kind of thing. And when people contacted me--
<v Anthony Holland>对不起。我的意思是，对我这个作曲家来说，就像如果某个小提琴手说，“嘿，你有一首给小提琴的曲子吗？”我说，“嗯，我有一个草图。它真的没什么大不了的，但我有一个小的开始。”“嗯，让我看看它是什么。”“当然。我会发给你。”所以这是一样的。当人们联系我时——

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:41.450
<v Jonathan Brody>You didn't send it as a sketch, Anthony. You sent them data that is as if we had done the proper controls. That's false advertising.
<v Jonathan Brody>你并没有把它当作草图发送，安东尼。你发送给他们的数据就好像我们已经做了适当的对照一样。那是虚假广告。

00:39:41.450 --> 00:40:10.380
<v Anthony Holland>Jonathan, in one case, I met for hours with the director of a medical research hospital in the cancer division. And I explained to this doctor exactly, exactly what we did. She knew it was preliminary. She knew there were holes and things that had to be nailed down. She knew all the details of everything, looked at the data, and said, "Yeah, let's go. Here's a seven-page contract." So I'm just saying--
<v Anthony Holland>乔纳森，在一种情况下，我会见了癌症部门的一家医学研究医院的院长几个小时。我向这位医生准确、准确地解释了我们做了什么。她知道它是初步的。她知道有漏洞和必须确定的事情。她了解一切细节，查看了数据，并说，“是的，我们走吧。这是一份七页的合同。”所以我只是说——

00:40:10.380 --> 00:40:13.320
<v Jonathan Brody>I just wanted to be part of the conversation.
<v Jonathan Brody>我只是想参与谈话。

00:40:13.320 --> 00:40:35.650
<v Anthony Holland>You asked me to respond. No scientist would take my preliminary data seriously. I'm sorry, that's wrong. And I think you need to make up your mind. Either this preliminary data I have is not worthy of another scientist's consideration, or you're mad at me for showing it to another scientist who considered it worthy.
<v Anthony Holland>你要求我回应。没有科学家会认真对待我的初步数据。对不起，那是错的。而且我认为你需要下定决心。要么我拥有的这些初步数据不值得另一位科学家的考虑，要么你因为我把它展示给另一位认为它值得的科学家而生我的气。

00:40:35.650 --> 00:40:42.680
<v Jonathan Brody>It's not. Anthony, we've had discussions. And you were mostly concerned about--
<v Jonathan Brody>它不值得。安东尼，我们讨论过。而你主要担心的是——

00:40:42.680 --> 00:41:03.550
<v Gabriel Rhodes>Finally, Jon and Anthony asked for time to talk alone with no microphone. After a while, they made up. But it doesn't seem like they're going to be working together anytime soon. What it felt like to sit in that room, it felt like watching a couple break up, which I felt more sad about than I'd expected to. They were this odd pair it was fun to root for.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>最后，乔恩和安东尼要求时间单独谈话，不带麦克风。过了一会儿，他们和好了。但看起来他们短期内不会在一起工作了。坐在那个房间里感觉就像看着一对情侣分手，这让我感到的悲伤比我预期的要多。他们是一对很难得的组合，为他们加油很有趣。

00:41:03.550 --> 00:41:22.610
<v Gabriel Rhodes>And on top of that, I just felt like, [BLEEP] cancer. Anthony's father died of cancer. Jon's aunt died of cancer. My mom died of cancer. Why can't we just get rid of this already? I think out of the three of us, only Jon, who deals with it every day, had realistic expectations when he started these experiments with Anthony.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>最重要的是，我只是觉得，[哔]癌症。安东尼的父亲死于癌症。乔恩的阿姨死于癌症。我的妈妈死于癌症。为什么我们不能早点摆脱这个？我认为在我们三个人中，只有每天都处理它的乔恩，在开始与安东尼的这些实验时有现实的期望。

00:41:22.610 --> 00:41:38.030
<v Jonathan Brody>Even if you have a good idea, even if you have a potential sci-fi cure, it's just not that easy. But if we want scientific breakthroughs, this is what it takes.
<v Jonathan Brody>即使你有一个好主意，即使你有一个潜在的科幻治愈方法，它就是没那么容易。但如果我们想要科学突破，这就是代价。

00:41:38.030 --> 00:41:48.030
<v Gabriel Rhodes>We are trying to have breakthroughs. We're just not succeeding most of the time.
<v Gabriel Rhodes>我们正在努力寻求突破。我们只是在大多数时候没有成功。

00:41:48.030 --> 00:41:59.330
<v Ira Glass>Gabriel Rhodes. He's going to continue filming Anthony and Jon if either of them ever starts this research again. You can see clips from what he's filmed so far at thecuredocumentary.com.
<v Ira Glass>加布里埃尔·罗兹。如果他们中的任何一个人再次开始这项研究，他将继续拍摄安东尼和乔恩。你可以在thecuredocumentary.com看到他到目前为止拍摄的片段。

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:14.060
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, trying something crazy, throwing a Hail Mary pass to solve a tough problem in your own personal life. A true story. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，尝试一些疯狂的事情，投掷一个万福玛丽球来解决你自己个人生活中的一个棘手问题。一个真实的故事。那一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:42:14.060 --> 00:42:27.590
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, So Crazy It Just Might Work, stories of people trying a risky thing that nobody ever tries. And that is for a good reason.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们选择一个主题，为你带来关于那个主题的各种故事。今天的节目，“太疯狂了，它可能真的会奏效”，人们尝试一件没人敢尝试的冒险事情的故事。而且这有一个很好的理由。

00:42:27.590 --> 00:42:51.660
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at Act Two of our show. Act Two, Benny Takes a Jet. So Benny was 23 in Utah. And he would want you to know, before you hear anything else in this story, that he knows that he was an immature 23, because he was gay and a Mormon, which meant that he did not admit to others or himself that he was gay. So he was utterly unused to how to handle a crush, which is what this story is about, a crush.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了我们节目的第二幕。第二幕，本尼搭乘喷气机。所以本尼在犹他州23岁。在你听到这个故事中的任何其他内容之前，他会希望你知道，他知道他是一个不成熟的23岁，因为他是同性恋而且是摩门教徒，这意味着他没有向别人或自己承认他是同性恋。所以他完全不习惯如何处理迷恋，这就是这个故事的主题，一个迷恋。

00:42:51.660 --> 00:43:01.070
<v Ira Glass>The crushee is 18 and Mormon. And Benny met him when he came in for a job interview. In retrospect, Benny says, he was obviously straight. Though at the job interview--
<v Ira Glass>迷恋的对象是18岁，摩门教徒。本尼在他来面试工作时见到了他。回顾过去，本尼说，他显然是异性恋。虽然在面试时——

00:43:01.070 --> 00:43:28.480
<v Benny>He was flirtatious with me in that he wanted the job really bad. So he was very complimentary towards me. He kept telling me, "You're, like, the coolest guy I've ever interviewed with my entire life. You're so cool. You're the kind of guy that everybody wants to hang out with. And you've got this energy kind of emulating." And I was just-- I was so taken by him. And he had these long, black eyelashes and these blue eyes. And I was just all gawking at him.
<v Benny>他和我调情，因为他非常想要这份工作。所以他非常赞美我。他一直告诉我，“你，就像，我一生中面试过的最酷的家伙。你太酷了。你是那种每个人都想一起玩的家伙。而且你有这种能量在模仿。”而我只是——我被他迷住了。他有这些又长又黑的睫毛和这些蓝色的眼睛。我只是在他面前目瞪口呆。

00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:33.160
<v Ira Glass>Really? Tell me everything you remember about stuff he said and stuff you said.
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？告诉我你记得的他说的东西和你说的东西。

00:43:33.160 --> 00:43:57.030
<v Benny>I remember saying to him, "You know, this isn't just a normal retail company. When people come through the door, it's very important to get intimate and just, like, lock eyes with people and make them feel-- like, really seduce them." And he's like, "Kind of like I'm seducing you right now?" And keep in mind, this boy is hopelessly straight. However, I was interpreting it the way I wanted it to go.
<v Benny>我记得对他说了，“你知道，这不仅仅是一个正常的零售公司。当人们走进门时，进行亲密接触并就像，锁定眼睛和让人们感到——就像，真的引诱他们是非常重要的。”他说，“就像我现在正在引诱你一样？”还要记住，这个男孩是无可救药的异性恋。然而，我正在按照我想要的方式解读它。

00:43:57.030 --> 00:44:10.200
<v Benny>And I remember, he kept doing that little-- every time it would be like a touche moment, he would reach over and hit me, kind of-- "Yeah, bro," kind of hit me on the knee. And it was making me crazy.
<v Benny>而且我记得，他一直在做那个小的——每次都是那种接触时刻，他会伸手击中我，就像——“是的，兄弟，”那种击中我的膝盖。这让我发疯。

00:44:10.200 --> 00:44:11.250
<v Ira Glass>Oh, he touched you.
<v Ira Glass>哦，他碰了你。

00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:12.890
<v Benny>Several times.
<v Benny>好几次。

00:44:12.890 --> 00:44:18.600
<v Ira Glass>So you hire him for this job. And then he's working there at the company. And how often would you see him?
